+Use the Cartesian derivative conventions $\nabla f=(\partial_i f)_{i=1}^3$, $\operatorname{div}u=\sum_i\partial_i u_i$, and $(u\cdot\nabla)u=\sum_j u_j\partial_j u$. The velocity has continuous first time, first spatial, and second spatial derivatives, and pressure has continuous first spatial derivatives. For this inviscid branch define the formal pressure stress by $\sigma_E=-pI_3$. For each fixed $t\in I$, set $p^{[t]}(x)=p(t,x)$ and $\sigma_E^{[t]}(x)=-p^{[t]}(x)I_3$. On a compact oriented regular surface patch $S\subset\Omega$ with unit normal $n_S$, define its formal stress resultant at time $t$ to be $\int_S\sigma_E^{[t]}n_S\,dS=-\int_Sp^{[t]} n_S\,dS$. For time-independent fields the resultant is independent of $t$, and the subscripts may be omitted. A steady solution has $\partial_tu=0$. These definitions impose no boundary condition, assert no solution existence, and do not claim that the system describes an observed fluid.
 
